# Art, music, drama and design, stages 0 to 2 (ages 3 to 13).
# Compare patterns, find relationships, assemble a composition, revise a design.

@ arts.0.colors
goal: Name colours, and know what happens when two colours are mixed.
intro: Red, yellow and blue are three colours that you can mix to make others. Mix two of them and you get a new colour.
  show: chips red | yellow | blue
demo: Put red paint and yellow paint together. Stir. You get orange!
  show: flow red and yellow > stir > orange
F: Mix the colours.
  frame: Red and yellow make __
  taps: orange | green | purple
  answer: orange
  x green :: [wrong-mix] Green comes from blue and yellow.
  x purple :: [wrong-mix] Purple comes from red and blue.
  hint: Think of the colour of a carrot.
  why: Red and yellow mixed together make orange.
F: Mix two more colours.
  frame: Blue and yellow make __
  taps: orange | green | purple
  answer: green
  x orange :: [wrong-mix] Orange comes from red and yellow.
  x purple :: [wrong-mix] Purple comes from red and blue.
  hint: Think of the colour of grass.
  why: Blue and yellow mixed together make green.
M: Match each thing to its colour.
  pairs: Banana => Yellow | Grass => Green | Sky => Blue | Strawberry => Red
  hint: What colour is the sky on a sunny day?
  why: Bananas are yellow, grass is green, the sky is blue and strawberries are red.
>P: You have red paint and blue paint. What colour can you make?
  + Purple :: Red and blue make purple.
  - Green :: [wrong-mix] Green needs yellow and blue.
  - Orange :: [wrong-mix] Orange needs red and yellow.
  hint: Think of the colour of a plum.
  why: Red and blue mixed together make purple.
done: Three colours can make many more. Try mixing with paints or crayons.

@ arts.0.drawing
goal: Make pictures from lines and simple shapes.
intro: Every picture is made of lines and shapes. Lines can be straight or curvy. Shapes can be circles, squares and triangles.
  show: shapes circle square triangle rectangle
demo: To draw a house, start with a square. Put a triangle on top for the roof. Add a rectangle for the door. Shapes make the picture.
  show: flow a square > a triangle on top > a door > a house
G: Straight or curvy?
  bin Straight: the edge of a ruler | a table top | a tall pole
  bin Curvy: a wave | a snake | a rainbow
  hint: Does it bend?
  why: Straight lines do not bend. Curvy lines bend and wiggle.
M: Match each shape to something you can draw with it.
  pairs: Circle => The Sun | Triangle => A roof | Square => A window | Rectangle => A door
  hint: Which shape is round?
  why: Simple shapes are the start of every drawing.
O: Put the steps in order to draw a house.
  items: Draw a square | Put a triangle on top | Add a door and windows | Colour it in
  ends: First | Last
  hint: Start with the biggest shape.
  why: Big shapes first, small parts next, colour last.
>P: You want to draw a cat. What should you look for first?
  + Its big shapes: a round head and an oval body :: Start with the big shapes.
  - Every single hair :: [details-first] The little details come last.
  - Nothing: just guess :: [did-not-look] Looking carefully helps you draw.
  hint: What did we draw first for the house?
  why: Artists look for the big shapes first and add the details afterwards.
done: Look for shapes in the things around you, and draw one today.

@ arts.0.singing
goal: Tell loud from quiet, clap a steady beat, and listen to music.
intro: Sounds can be loud or quiet. Sounds can be high or low. Music has a steady beat that you can clap along to.
  show: emoji 🎵 👏 👂
demo: Listen: clap, clap, clap, clap. Each clap is the same distance from the next, like the tick of a clock. That steady clapping is the beat.
  show: chips clap | clap | clap | clap
G: Loud or quiet?
  bin Loud: a drum | thunder | a shout
  bin Quiet: a whisper | a mouse | falling snow
  hint: Would it wake a sleeping baby?
  why: Drums, thunder and shouts are loud. Whispers, mice and snow are quiet.
P: Clap, clap, clap, clap: all steady, like a ticking clock. What is this called?
  + The beat :: The beat is the steady pulse of the music.
  - A whisper :: [mixed-up-words] A whisper is a quiet voice.
  - A colour :: [mixed-up-words] Colours are for seeing. The beat is for hearing.
  hint: It is the steady part that you tap your foot to.
  why: The beat is the steady pulse in music.
O: Get ready to sing. Put the steps in order.
  items: Stand tall | Take a big breath | Listen for the start | Sing
  ends: First | Last
  hint: You need air before you can sing.
  why: Stand, breathe, listen, sing.
>P: The music gets faster. What should your claps do?
  + Get faster too :: Your claps follow the beat.
  - Stay slow :: [ignored-the-beat] Then your claps would not fit the music.
  - Stop :: [gave-up] Keep clapping and follow the music.
  hint: Your claps go with the beat.
  why: When the beat speeds up, we clap faster to stay with it.
done: Clap along to a song you like, and feel the beat.

@ arts.0.dance
goal: Move in time with music, fast or slow, big or small.
intro: When we hear music, our bodies want to move. Fast music makes us move quickly. Slow music makes us move slowly.
  show: emoji 🎶 💃 🕺
demo: The drum goes boom, boom, boom. Step, step, step: one step for each boom. You are dancing on the beat.
  show: flow boom > step > boom > step
M: Match the music to the way you move.
  pairs: Fast music => Quick steps | Slow music => Slow, smooth moves | Loud music => Big moves | Quiet music => Small, soft moves
  hint: How would you move to a lullaby?
  why: Dancers make their movements fit the music.
G: A fast move or a slow move?
  bin Fast: running on the spot | spinning round | hopping quickly
  bin Slow: tiptoeing | swaying | stretching up high
  hint: Which ones make you puff?
  why: Running, spinning and hopping are fast. Tiptoeing, swaying and stretching are slow.
P: You are playing musical statues. The music stops. What do you do?
  + Stand as still as a statue :: When the music stops, you stop.
  - Keep on dancing :: [did-not-listen] The game is to stop when the music stops.
  - Shout :: [did-not-listen] The game is to keep still and quiet.
  hint: What does a statue do?
  why: Dancing means listening. When the music stops, the dance stops.
>P: The drum goes boom… boom… boom, slow and heavy. How could you move?
  + Big, slow stomps, like an elephant :: Slow and heavy, to fit the drum.
  - Tiny fast steps, like a mouse :: [ignored-the-beat] The drum is slow and heavy, not quick and light.
  - Not at all :: [gave-up] Listen to the drum and move with it.
  hint: Which animal is slow and heavy?
  why: A slow, heavy beat suits slow, heavy movements.
done: Put on some music and move the way it sounds.

@ arts.1.elements
goal: Find line, shape, colour, texture and pattern in a picture.
intro: Artists use five building blocks: line, shape, colour, texture and pattern. You can find them in every picture.
  show: chips line | shape | colour | texture | pattern
demo: Look at a zebra. Its stripes are lines. The stripes repeat, so they make a pattern. Its coat looks soft, and that is texture.
  show: table What you see | What it is called ;; stripes | lines ;; stripes that repeat | pattern ;; a soft coat | texture
M: Match each word to what it means.
  pairs: Line => A mark that goes from one place to another | Shape => A flat area with an edge | Texture => How a thing feels, or looks as if it feels | Pattern => Something that repeats
  hint: Which word is about touching?
  why: These are the words that artists use for the parts of a picture.
G: Rough or smooth?
  bin Rough: tree bark | sandpaper | a brick
  bin Smooth: glass | a pebble | silk
  hint: Think about how each one would feel.
  why: Texture is how a thing feels. Bark is rough and glass is smooth.
G: Warm colours or cool colours?
  bin Warm: red | orange | yellow
  bin Cool: blue | green | purple
  hint: Which colours are like fire? Which are like water?
  why: Reds, oranges and yellows feel warm, like fire. Blues, greens and purples feel cool, like water.
P: Which of these is a pattern?
  + Red, blue, red, blue, red, blue :: It repeats.
  - Red, blue, green, yellow, pink :: [no-repeat] Nothing repeats, so it is not a pattern.
  hint: A pattern repeats.
  why: A pattern is made when something repeats.
>P: An artist wants a picture to feel cold, like winter. Which colours should she choose?
  + Blues and whites :: Cool colours feel cold.
  - Reds and oranges :: [warm-for-cold] Those are warm colours, like fire.
  - Every colour at once :: [no-choice] Choosing a few cool colours gives the cold feeling.
  hint: Which colours are cool?
  why: Artists choose colours for the feeling they give. Cool colours feel cold and calm.
done: Look at a picture in a book and find a line, a shape and a pattern.

@ arts.1.crafts
goal: Know different ways of making art with your hands, and plan a model that stands up.
intro: Art is not only drawing. You can stick, cut, shape, weave and build. Some art is flat. Some art you can walk all the way round.
  show: chips collage | sculpture | weaving | pottery
demo: To make a pinch pot, roll clay into a ball. Press your thumb into the middle. Pinch the sides, turning as you go, until they are thin. Now you have a little bowl.
  show: flow a ball of clay > press your thumb in > pinch the sides > a pot
M: Match each kind of craft to what it is.
  pairs: Sculpture => Art that you can walk round | Collage => A picture made by sticking pieces down | Weaving => Threads going over and under | Pottery => Things shaped from clay
  hint: Which one uses clay? Which one uses glue?
  why: Each craft has its own materials and its own way of working.
G: Flat, or with a front, a back and sides?
  bin Flat: a drawing | a painting | a collage
  bin Has sides: a sculpture | a clay pot | a model
  hint: Could you walk round it and see the back?
  why: Drawings and paintings are flat. Sculptures and models have a front, a back and sides.
O: Put the steps in order to make a pinch pot.
  items: Roll the clay into a ball | Press your thumb into the middle | Pinch the sides thin | Leave it to dry | Paint it
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The clay must be dry before you paint it.
  why: Ball, thumb, pinch, dry, paint.
>P: You want to make a model of a tall giraffe that stands up by itself. What matters most?
  + Strong legs and a wide base :: Then it will not fall over.
  - Painting the spots first :: [decoration-first] First make it stand. Decorate it afterwards.
  - Making it as thin as you can :: [ignored-balance] A thin model with a long neck will topple.
  hint: Why do tall things fall over?
  why: Makers think first about how a thing will stand, and then about how it will look.
done: Make something with your hands this week: a collage, a pot or a model.

@ arts.1.instruments
goal: Sort musical instruments into families by how they make their sound.
intro: Instruments belong to families. Some have strings. Some you blow. Some you hit or shake.
  show: table Family | How the sound is made ;; strings | a string is plucked or bowed ;; wind | you blow ;; percussion | you hit or shake
demo: A violin has four strings. When the bow rubs a string, the string shakes very fast. The shaking makes the sound. Every sound comes from something shaking.
  show: flow the bow rubs the string > the string shakes > we hear a sound
G: Which family?
  bin Strings: violin | guitar | harp
  bin Wind: flute | trumpet | recorder
  bin Percussion: drum | tambourine | xylophone
  hint: Do you pluck it, blow it, or hit it?
  why: Violins, guitars and harps have strings. Flutes, trumpets and recorders are blown. Drums, tambourines and xylophones are hit or shaken.
M: Match each instrument to how you play it.
  pairs: Violin => A bow rubs the strings | Drum => You hit the skin | Flute => You blow across a hole | Guitar => You pluck the strings
  hint: Which one needs a bow?
  why: Each instrument has its own way of starting the sound.
P: Which of these makes the lowest sound?
  + A double bass :: It is the biggest, with the longest strings.
  - A violin :: [size-and-pitch] A violin is small, and so its sound is high.
  - A viola :: [size-and-pitch] A viola is a little bigger than a violin, but far smaller than a double bass.
  hint: Which is the biggest?
  why: Big instruments make low sounds. Small instruments make high sounds.
>P: You shake a new instrument, and beads rattle inside it. Which family does it belong to?
  + Percussion :: You shake it to make the sound.
  - Strings :: [wrong-family] It has no strings.
  - Wind :: [wrong-family] You do not blow it.
  hint: Which family do you hit or shake?
  why: Anything that you hit or shake to make a sound is percussion.
done: Listen to a piece of music and try to name one instrument.

@ arts.1.beat
goal: Tell the beat from the rhythm, and follow a tune as it goes up and down.
intro: Music has a steady beat, like a heartbeat. On top of the beat, sounds can be long or short: that is the rhythm. The tune, which goes up and down, is the melody.
  show: table Word | Meaning ;; beat | the steady pulse ;; rhythm | the pattern of long and short sounds ;; melody | the tune that goes up and down ;; tempo | how fast the music goes
demo: Count "ONE, two, three, four" again and again, tapping each number. That is the beat, in groups of four. Now sing a song over it. The words go faster and slower, but the beat stays the same.
  show: chips ONE | two | three | four
M: Match each word to its meaning.
  pairs: Beat => The steady pulse | Rhythm => The pattern of long and short sounds | Melody => The tune that goes up and down | Tempo => How fast or slow the music goes
  hint: Which one do you tap your foot to?
  why: Beat, rhythm, melody and tempo are four different things that you can hear in music.
F: Count the beats: ONE, two, three, four. ONE, two, three, four.
  frame: There are __ beats in each group
  taps: 2 | 3 | 4
  answer: 4
  x 3 :: [miscounted] Count again: one, two, three, four.
  x 2 :: [miscounted] Count again: one, two, three, four.
  hint: Count up to the next "ONE".
  why: The beats come in groups of four, and the first beat of each group is the strongest.
O: Put the notes in order, from the lowest to the highest.
  items: do | re | mi | fa | sol
  ends: Lowest | Highest
  hint: Sing them, going up one step at a time.
  why: Do, re, mi, fa, sol: each note is a step higher than the one before.
>P: You clap once for every sound in the words "Happy birthday to you". Some claps are quick and some are slow. Is that the beat or the rhythm?
  + The rhythm :: It follows the long and short sounds of the words.
  - The beat :: [beat-and-rhythm-confused] The beat is steady. These claps are sometimes quick and sometimes slow.
  - The tempo :: [wrong-word] Tempo is how fast the whole song goes.
  hint: Were your claps all the same distance apart?
  why: Clapping the words gives the rhythm. Tapping steadily gives the beat.
done: Tap the beat with your foot and clap the rhythm with your hands.

@ arts.2.art-history-intro
goal: Place the great periods of art in order, and recognise each by how it looks.
intro: People have made pictures for at least forty thousand years. The way they made them has changed again and again, as their ideas, materials and purposes changed.
  show: time 17,000 years ago=cave paintings | 1400 BCE=Egyptian tomb paintings | 1500=the Renaissance | 1870=Impressionism | 1910=Cubism
demo: A medieval painter set flat figures against gold and made the most important person the largest. A Renaissance painter made the people look solid and drew distant things smaller, so that the picture looks like a view through a window.
  show: table Medieval | Renaissance ;; a flat gold background | deep space ;; size shows importance | size shows distance
O: Put these kinds of art in order, from the oldest to the newest.
  items: Cave paintings | Egyptian tomb paintings | Renaissance paintings | Impressionist paintings | Cubist paintings
  ends: Oldest | Newest
  hint: Cave paintings are by far the oldest. Cubism belongs to the twentieth century.
  why: Many thousands of years separate the caves from Egypt. The last three all fall within about four hundred years.
M: Match each kind of art to how it looks.
  pairs: Cave art => Animals painted on rock in earth colours | Egyptian art => Figures shown side-on, in flat rows | Renaissance art => Lifelike people in deep, realistic space | Impressionism => Quick dabs of colour that catch the light | Cubism => Things broken into flat shapes, seen from several sides
  hint: Which one tries to show a thing from several sides at once?
  why: Each period had its own way of seeing, and so its own look.
C: Compare the two ways of painting a crowd.
  left: text The king is drawn twice as tall as everyone else, against flat gold.
  right: text The people nearest to us are drawn larger, and those far away smaller.
  + The left uses size to show importance; the right uses size to show distance :: Two different rules for the same choice.
  - The left is a mistake by an unskilled painter :: [judged-by-later-rules] It follows a different rule on purpose: importance, not distance.
  - They follow the same rule :: [missed-the-difference] In one, size means rank. In the other, it means distance.
  hint: Why is the king so large in the left-hand picture?
  why: Artists of different times worked by different rules. Perspective, in which distant things are drawn smaller, was worked out in the Renaissance.
>P: A painting shows a haystack at sunset in loose dabs of orange and violet. It was painted quickly, outdoors. Which kind of art is it?
  + Impressionism :: Quick dabs of colour, catching the light of one moment.
  - Cubism :: [wrong-style] Cubism breaks things into flat geometric shapes.
  - Egyptian art :: [wrong-style] Egyptian art shows flat figures in neat rows.
  hint: Which painters cared most about light and the passing moment?
  why: The Impressionists painted outdoors to catch how light looked at a particular time of day.
done: Every age sees differently. Find one painting from each period and compare them.

@ arts.2.color-theory
goal: Use the colour wheel, contrast, balance and perspective to plan a picture.
intro: Colours are related, and the colour wheel shows how. Colours opposite each other on the wheel are called complementary. Placed side by side, each makes the other look its brightest.
  show: table Colour | Its opposite on the wheel ;; red | green ;; blue | orange ;; yellow | purple
demo: Railway lines seem to come together in the distance, although they are always the same distance apart. The point where they seem to meet is the vanishing point. Drawing things smaller as they near that point makes a flat picture look deep.
  show: flow near: large > farther: smaller > the vanishing point
M: Match each colour to its complement.
  pairs: Red => Green | Blue => Orange | Yellow => Purple
  hint: The complement is made by mixing the other two primary colours.
  why: Each primary colour sits opposite the mixture of the other two.
P: In a drawing of a long straight road, the two edges seem to meet far away. What is that point called?
  + The vanishing point :: Where parallel lines seem to meet.
  - The centre of interest :: [wrong-term] That is wherever the picture draws the eye. It need not be where the lines meet.
  - The horizon :: [near-miss] The vanishing point lies on the horizon, but the horizon is the whole line.
  hint: Things seem to vanish there.
  why: Lines that are really parallel are drawn meeting at the vanishing point. That is the basis of perspective.
C: Which arrangement is balanced?
  left: text One large dark shape on the left, and nothing on the right.
  right: text One large dark shape on the left, and three small bright shapes on the right.
  + The right: the small bright shapes balance the large dark one :: Balance does not need the two sides to match.
  - The left: it is simpler :: [simple-means-balanced] All the weight is on one side, so it feels as if it is tipping.
  - Neither, because the two sides do not match :: [balance-means-symmetry] A picture can be balanced without being symmetrical.
  hint: Think of a seesaw: one heavy child against several light ones.
  why: Different things can balance one another by size, brightness and position.
E: The words on this poster cannot be read from across the room. Revise it.
  tokens: Poster: | red words{white words,dark red words} | on a | red background
  limit: 1
  aim: Change one thing so that the words stand out.
  accept: 2=white words
  x 2=dark red words :: [too-little-contrast] Dark red on red is still hard to read.
  hint: Words stand out when they differ strongly from what is behind them.
  why: Contrast makes things visible. White on red is strong; red on red disappears.
>P: A painter wants an orange to stand out as strongly as possible. Which colour should the background be?
  + Blue :: Blue is the complement of orange.
  - Orange :: [same-colour] The fruit would disappear into it.
  - Red-orange :: [neighbouring-colour] Colours close together on the wheel blend. They do not contrast.
  hint: Which colour is opposite orange on the wheel?
  why: Complementary colours make each other look most vivid.
done: Opposites stand out and neighbours blend. Plan a picture using one pair of complementary colours.

@ arts.2.music-reading
goal: Read notes on the treble stave, count note lengths and understand a time signature.
intro: Music is written on five lines called a stave. The higher a note sits on the stave, the higher it sounds. The shape of a note shows how long it lasts.
  show: table Note | Beats ;; whole note (semibreve) | 4 ;; half note (minim) | 2 ;; quarter note (crotchet) | 1 ;; eighth note (quaver) | a half
demo: A time signature of 4/4 means four quarter-note beats in every bar. A bar may be filled by one whole note, or two half notes, or four quarter notes, or any mixture that adds up to four.
  show: table A bar of 4/4 | Adds up to ;; one whole note | 4 ;; two half notes | 2 + 2 ;; a half note and two quarter notes | 2 + 1 + 1
M: Match each note to its length.
  pairs: Whole note => 4 beats | Half note => 2 beats | Quarter note => 1 beat | Eighth note => Half a beat
  hint: Each one lasts half as long as the one before.
  why: Each note value is half of the one above it: 4, 2, 1 and a half.
O: The lines of the treble stave spell a pattern. Put the notes in order, from the bottom line to the top.
  items: E | G | B | D | F
  ends: Bottom line | Top line
  hint: "Every Good Boy Deserves Fruit."
  why: From the bottom, the lines of the treble stave are E, G, B, D and F.
F: Add up the notes.
  frame: A half note and two quarter notes last __ beats altogether
  answer: 4 | four
  x 3 :: [counted-the-notes] There are three notes, but they last 2 + 1 + 1 beats.
  hint: 2 + 1 + 1.
  why: 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 beats: exactly one bar of 4/4.
P: What does the time signature 3/4 mean?
  + Three quarter-note beats in each bar :: The top number counts the beats, and the bottom number says what kind.
  - Three bars of four beats :: [misread-the-numbers] The numbers describe a single bar.
  - Play at three-quarters of the speed :: [read-as-a-fraction] It is not a fraction of the speed. It tells you how the beats are grouped.
  hint: The top number says how many beats, and the bottom number says which note gets one beat.
  why: In 3/4 each bar has three quarter-note beats, as in a waltz.
>P: A bar in 3/4 already contains a half note. Which note completes the bar?
  + One quarter note :: 2 + 1 = 3 beats.
  - One half note :: [overfilled] 2 + 2 = 4 beats, which is too many for 3/4.
  - One whole note :: [overfilled] A whole note by itself is 4 beats.
  hint: How many beats are still missing?
  why: Each bar must add up exactly to the number of beats in the time signature.
done: Lines, lengths and bars. Clap the rhythm of the first line of a piece you know.

@ arts.2.theatre
goal: Explain how a play is made and performed, and show a character's feelings through voice and movement.
intro: A play is a story told by actors in front of an audience. It begins as a script. A director, the actors and a backstage crew turn the words into a performance.
  show: table Word | Meaning ;; script | the written words and directions ;; stage directions | instructions for movement and manner ;; props | the objects that actors use ;; set | the scenery
demo: Say "I never said she took my book" seven times, stressing a different word each time. "I never said…" means that someone else said it. "…my book" means that she took something else. The same words carry seven meanings.
  show: steps I never said she took my book: somebody else said it ;; I never said SHE took my book: somebody else took it ;; I never said she took my BOOK: she took something else
M: Match each word of the theatre to its meaning.
  pairs: Script => The written words of the play | Director => The person who guides the performance | Props => Objects that the actors use | Set => The scenery on the stage | Stage directions => Written instructions for movement and manner
  hint: A prop can be picked up; a set cannot.
  why: These are the working words of the theatre. Everyone in a production uses them.
C: An actor says, "I never said she took my book", in two ways. How do the meanings differ?
  left: text I never said SHE took my book.
  right: text I never said she took my BOOK.
  + The left suggests that someone else took it; the right suggests that she took something else :: Stress changes the meaning.
  - They mean the same, since the words are the same :: [words-only] In speech, emphasis is part of the meaning.
  - The left is simply louder :: [volume-only] The stress points to what is being denied.
  hint: Which word is being corrected in each?
  why: Actors choose which word to stress, because the stress tells the audience what the character means.
O: Put the stages of putting on a play in order.
  items: Choose the script | Cast the parts | Rehearse | Hold a dress rehearsal | Perform on opening night
  ends: First | Last
  hint: The actors must be chosen before they can rehearse.
  why: Script, cast, rehearsals, dress rehearsal, performance.
P: On a stage, where is "upstage"?
  + At the back, farthest from the audience :: Stages once sloped upwards towards the back.
  - At the front, nearest the audience :: [reversed] That is downstage.
  - On the actor's left :: [wrong-direction] That is stage left.
  hint: Old stages sloped, with the back higher than the front.
  why: Upstage is away from the audience and downstage is towards it.
>P: An actor must show that her character is afraid, without saying so. What should she do?
  + Use her voice, face and body: back away, glance about, let her voice shake :: Showing, not telling.
  - Announce, "I am afraid" :: [told-not-shown] The task is to show it without saying it.
  - Stand still with a blank face :: [showed-nothing] The audience would have nothing to read.
  hint: How do people look and sound when they are frightened?
  why: Acting shows feeling through what a character does. The audience reads the voice, the face and the movement.
done: The same words carry many meanings. Read one line aloud in three different ways.

@ arts.2.photography
goal: Frame a picture deliberately, choose a camera angle for its effect, and explain how a film is put together.
intro: A photograph is a choice: what to include, where to stand and where to place the subject in the frame. A film is thousands of such choices, joined together.
  show: table Shot | Effect ;; wide shot | shows the whole place ;; close-up | shows feeling in a face ;; low angle | makes the subject look powerful ;; high angle | makes the subject look small
demo: Imagine the frame divided into thirds each way, like a board for noughts and crosses. Placing the subject on one of the lines, and not in the dead centre, usually makes a livelier picture. This is called the rule of thirds.
  show: grid 3x3 on=4
M: Match each shot to its effect.
  pairs: Wide shot => Shows the whole place | Close-up => Shows the feeling in a face | Low angle => Makes the subject look powerful | High angle => Makes the subject look small
  hint: Looking up at someone makes them seem big.
  why: Where the camera stands changes how the audience feels about the subject.
C: Which framing is usually the stronger?
  left: text The horizon cuts the picture exactly in half, and the person stands dead centre.
  right: text The horizon lies a third of the way down, and the person stands to one side, looking into the space.
  + The right: off-centre placing gives the picture movement, and gives the person room to look into :: The rule of thirds.
  - The left: the centre is always the best place :: [centre-is-best] A centred subject can look static.
  - It makes no difference :: [framing-ignored] Where things sit in the frame changes how the picture feels.
  hint: Which picture gives your eye somewhere to travel?
  why: Placing the subject off-centre, with space in the direction they face, makes a picture feel alive.
F: A film is a series of still pictures shown very quickly: 24 every second.
  frame: Two seconds of film contain __ pictures
  answer: 48
  x 24 :: [one-second-only] That is one second. Two seconds need twice as many.
  x 26 :: [added-instead] Multiply: 24 pictures for each of the two seconds.
  hint: 24 for each second.
  why: 2 × 24 = 48. Shown quickly enough, still pictures appear to move.
O: Put the stages of making a film in order.
  items: Write the script | Plan the shots on a storyboard | Film the scenes | Edit the shots together | Add sound and music
  ends: First | Last
  hint: Planning comes before filming, and editing comes after.
  why: Script, storyboard, filming, editing, sound.
>P: A director wants the audience to feel that a villain is powerful and threatening. Which shot should she choose?
  + A low angle, looking up at the villain :: Looking up makes a figure tower over us.
  - A high angle, looking down on the villain :: [reversed] Looking down makes a figure seem small and weak.
  - A wide shot with the villain tiny in the distance :: [wrong-scale] A tiny figure does not threaten.
  hint: How does it feel to look up at someone very tall?
  why: The camera angle tells the audience how to feel about a character.
done: Every picture is a choice. Take three photographs of one object from three different angles.

@ arts.2.music-grade-1
goal: Read notes in the treble and bass clefs, count simple time, and build the major scales of C, G, D and F.
intro: A major scale is a ladder of eight notes with a fixed pattern of steps. The key signature at the start of each line shows which notes are sharp or flat throughout the piece.
  show: table Key | Key signature ;; C major | no sharps or flats ;; G major | F sharp ;; D major | F sharp and C sharp ;; F major | B flat
demo: Play the white notes from C up to the next C: C, D, E, F, G, A, B, C. That is C major. The steps are all whole tones except between the 3rd and 4th notes and between the 7th and 8th, which are semitones. Every major scale keeps this pattern.
  show: table Step | 1 to 2 | 2 to 3 | 3 to 4 | 4 to 5 ;; Size | tone | tone | semitone | tone
M: Match each key to its key signature.
  pairs: C major => No sharps or flats | G major => One sharp: F sharp | D major => Two sharps: F sharp and C sharp | F major => One flat: B flat
  hint: Only one of these keys uses a flat.
  why: Each key needs its sharps or flats to keep the pattern of tones and semitones.
B: Build the scale of G major, going up from G.
  bank: G | A | B | C | D | E | F sharp | F | B flat
  answer: G | A | B | C | D | E | F sharp
  x F :: [missed-the-sharp] G major needs F sharp, so that the last step is a semitone.
  x B flat :: [wrong-key] B flat belongs to F major.
  hint: Use every letter once, and remember the key signature of G major.
  why: G, A, B, C, D, E, F sharp. The F sharp makes a semitone between the 7th note and the top G.
F: Find the semitones in a major scale.
  frame: The semitones lie between the 3rd and 4th notes, and between the __th and 8th
  answer: 7 | seven | seventh
  x 6 :: [wrong-degree] From the 6th to the 7th is a whole tone.
  hint: The last step of the scale is a small one.
  why: Tone, tone, semitone, tone, tone, tone, semitone: the semitones fall at 3–4 and 7–8.
P: Which note sits on the top line of the bass stave?
  + A :: The lines of the bass stave are G, B, D, F and A.
  - F :: [read-as-treble] F is the top line of the treble stave.
  - G :: [wrong-end] G is the bottom line of the bass stave.
  hint: The lines of the bass stave, from the bottom: "Good Boys Deserve Fruit Always."
  why: The bass clef names its lines G, B, D, F, A, so the top line is A.
>P: A melody uses the notes F, G, A, B flat and C, and ends on F. Which key is it in?
  + F major :: It has one flat, B flat, and comes to rest on F.
  - C major :: [missed-the-flat] C major has no flats.
  - G major :: [wrong-accidental] G major has an F sharp, and no B flat.
  hint: Which key has just one flat?
  why: A key is shown by its sharps or flats and by the note on which the music comes to rest.
done: One pattern, many keys. Play or sing each scale and listen for the two semitones.

@ arts.2.music-grade-2
goal: Tell minor from major, find the relative minor of a key, and count triplets and a wider range of simple time signatures.
intro: Every major key has a relative minor that shares its key signature. The minor key begins on the sixth note of the major scale. Its smaller third gives it a darker sound.
  show: table Major key | Relative minor | Key signature ;; C major | A minor | none ;; G major | E minor | one sharp ;; F major | D minor | one flat
demo: From C up to E is a major third: four semitones. From C up to E flat is a minor third: three semitones. That one semitone is the main difference between the bright sound of major and the darker sound of minor.
  show: table Third | From | To | Semitones ;; major | C | E | 4 ;; minor | C | E flat | 3
M: Match each major key to its relative minor.
  pairs: C major => A minor | G major => E minor | F major => D minor
  hint: Count down three semitones from the major key note.
  why: The relative minor starts on the sixth note of the major scale, and shares its key signature.
C: Compare the two thirds.
  left: text C up to E: four semitones
  right: text C up to E flat: three semitones
  + The left is a major third and the right a minor third; the smaller one gives the minor sound :: One semitone changes the character.
  - They are the same interval, written differently :: [same-letter-names] Both are thirds, but one is a semitone smaller.
  - The right is a second :: [miscounted-letters] C to E of any kind spans three letter-names: a third.
  hint: Count the semitones in each.
  why: The quality of the third above the key note decides whether a chord or scale is major or minor.
P: In the harmonic minor scale of A minor, which note is raised?
  + G, to G sharp :: The seventh note is raised a semitone.
  - F, to F sharp :: [wrong-degree] In the harmonic minor only the seventh note is raised.
  - C, to C sharp :: [changed-the-third] Raising the third would turn the scale major.
  hint: It is the note just below the key note.
  why: Raising the seventh gives a semitone step up to the key note, which makes the music pull strongly towards home.
F: A triplet fits three equal notes into the time of two.
  frame: A triplet of quavers lasts __ crotchet beat
  answer: 1 | one
  x 3 :: [counted-the-notes] There are three notes, but together they fill one beat.
  x 1.5 :: [added-the-quavers] Three ordinary quavers would last a beat and a half. A triplet squeezes them into one.
  hint: Three in the time of two quavers, and two quavers make one crotchet.
  why: Two quavers make a crotchet, and a triplet of quavers takes the same time.
G: Sort the time signatures by the number of beats in a bar.
  bin Two beats: 2/4 | 2/2
  bin Three beats: 3/4 | 3/8
  bin Four beats: 4/4 | 4/2
  hint: The top number tells you how many beats there are.
  why: The top number gives the count. The bottom number says which kind of note gets one beat.
>P: A piece has one sharp in its key signature, often uses D sharp, and ends on E. Which key is it in?
  + E minor :: One sharp, a raised seventh (D sharp), and a close on E.
  - G major :: [ignored-the-accidental-and-the-ending] G major shares the key signature, but has no D sharp and would end on G.
  - D major :: [wrong-signature] D major has two sharps.
  hint: Which minor key shares a key signature with G major?
  why: A raised seventh and the final note tell a minor key from its relative major.
done: Same key signature, different home note. Play a tune in C major, and then start it on A.

@ arts.2.music-grade-3
goal: Read compound time, move a melody by an octave, and recognise keys with up to four sharps or flats.
intro: In simple time each beat divides into two. In compound time each beat divides into three, and the beat is a dotted note. Six-eight has two such beats to a bar.
  show: table Time signature | Beats in a bar | Each beat divides into ;; 2/4 | 2 | two quavers ;; 6/8 | 2 | three quavers ;; 9/8 | 3 | three quavers ;; 12/8 | 4 | three quavers
demo: Three-four and six-eight both hold six quavers in a bar, but they are grouped differently. In 3/4 they fall into three pairs: ONE-and TWO-and THREE-and. In 6/8 they fall into two threes: ONE-two-three FOUR-five-six. The same notes can sound like a waltz or like a jig.
  show: table Time | Grouping of six quavers ;; 3/4 | 2 + 2 + 2 ;; 6/8 | 3 + 3
G: Simple time or compound time?
  bin Simple: 2/4 | 3/4 | 4/4
  bin Compound: 6/8 | 9/8 | 12/8
  hint: In compound time the top number is 6, 9 or 12.
  why: Simple beats divide into two and compound beats into three.
F: Count the beats, not the quavers.
  frame: A bar of 6/8 has __ dotted-crotchet beats
  answer: 2 | two
  x 6 :: [counted-the-quavers] There are six quavers, grouped into two beats of three.
  x 3 :: [counted-as-simple] Three beats of two quavers would be 3/4.
  hint: The six quavers are grouped in threes.
  why: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 beats, each one a dotted crotchet.
O: Sharps are added to key signatures in a fixed order. Put the first four in order.
  items: F sharp | C sharp | G sharp | D sharp
  ends: First | Fourth
  hint: Each new sharp is five notes higher than the one before.
  why: F, C, G, D: each a fifth above the last. The flats are added in the opposite order.
M: Match each key signature to its major key.
  pairs: Three sharps => A major | Four sharps => E major | Two flats => B flat major | Three flats => E flat major | Four flats => A flat major
  hint: For sharp keys, the key note is a semitone above the last sharp.
  why: With sharps, go one semitone above the last sharp. With flats, the key is named by the last flat but one.
>P: Three-four and six-eight both hold six quavers in a bar. How do they differ?
  + 3/4 has three beats of two quavers; 6/8 has two beats of three :: The grouping, and so the feel, is different.
  - They do not differ at all :: [same-total-same-metre] The total is the same, but the beats are different.
  - 6/8 is simply played twice as fast :: [speed-not-grouping] The time signature sets the grouping, not the speed.
  hint: Where do the strong beats fall in each?
  why: Metre is about how the beats group. Six quavers can be three twos or two threes.
done: Count the beats, not the notes. Clap six quavers first as 3/4 and then as 6/8.
